egory" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070598/state.json" "file_sha256" "c846dc39001740d02f2ca1adfcf827d162e95a14cf757d2349903133274cea7e"} {"question" "q-321369" "field" "field-6" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070598/state.json" "file_sha256" "c846dc39001740d02f2ca1adfcf827d162e95a14cf757d2349903133274cea7e"} {"question" "q-321369" "field" "field-6" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070598/state.json" "file_sha256" "c846dc39001740d02f2ca1adfcf827d162e95a14cf757d2349903133274cea7e"} {"question" "e-9042" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The second-order differential equation \n\n$$\ny''  - 7t y' + e^{t}y= -t\n$$\n\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=f(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x_1(t) = y(t)$ and $x_2(t) = y'(t).$ Find the function $f(t).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070598/example-9042.json" "file_sha256" "7aff39e838d76fbc6fdcc87204e568b5fac8ce7ec7ea4c71f0dd9c8eef74f132"} {"question" "e-9042" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we make the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =y \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & ={y}^{′}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we're almost done. We just need to write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of $x_1$ and $x_2.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $(y''),$ we obtain\n\n$$\ny'' = 7t y' - e^{t}y - t.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\ny'' = 7t x_2 -e^{t}x_1 - t.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=7t{x}_{2}-{e}^{t}{x}_{1}-t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nf(t) = -e^{t}x_1 + 7t x_2 - t.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070598/example-9042.json" "file_sha256" "7aff39e838d76fbc6fdcc87204e568b5fac8ce7ec7ea4c71f0dd9c8eef74f132"} {"question" "e-9139" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the third-order differential equation\n\n$$\ny''' + t y' - 8 y = 1 - t^2,\n$$\n\nwhere $y = y(t).$ If\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y={x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}={x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {y}^{″}={x}_{3}(t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthen the given differential equation can be rewritten as the matrix equation $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t) + \\mathbf f(t),$ where\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070598/assets/2b3ba6edd838ee516ad902008726afbca516ab15892aad0f236c5a2270261985.png)\nDetermine the matrices $A$ and $\\mathbf{f}(t).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070598/example-9139.json" "file_sha256" "7aa737d2f5dc6d64222c1be092116c191bd466a252104d5a585ee03fcf89316c"} {"question" "e-9139" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We first need to express the given differential equation as a system of first-order differential equations. Then, we write the system as a matrix equation.\n\nWe start by making the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =y \\\\ {x}_{2} & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{3} & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′} & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′} & ={y}^{″} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′} & ={y}^{‴}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}={y}^{‴}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must now write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of $x_1, x_2$ and $x_3.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $(y'''),$ we obtain\n\n$$\ny''' = - t y' + 8 y + 1 - t^2.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\ny''' = - t x_2 + 8 x_1 + 1 - t^2.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}=8{x}_{1}-t{x}_{2}+1-{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can represent this system as the matrix differential equation $\\mathbf x'(t) = A\\mathbf x(t) + \\mathbf f(t)$ as follows:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070598/assets/5a343acd288c1f6ddc72320b4eb17d9f24d3d41adebec579e70b9ff7ba17d0bb.png)\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070598/assets/dcb089aa64d2aeb959e6b33b03afecef75de96962a0468eb31b590ed6ecad6eb.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070598/example-9139.json" "file_sha256" "7aa737d2f5dc6d64222c1be092116c191bd466a252104d5a585ee03fcf89316c"}] "usage" {"e-9042" [] "q-321369" [] "q-321304" [] "q-107932" [] "e-9139" []} "basis" 2496 "retractions" [[17592186176144 :question/problem "The third-order differential equation\n\n$y^{‴} - \\sqrt{t}y^{″} - y = t^{2}$\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n${x_{1}^{′} = x_{2}; x_{2}^{′} = x_{3}; x_{3}^{′} = f(t)$\nwhere $x_{1}(t) = y(t)$, $x_{2}(t) = y^{′}(t)$ and $x_{3}(t) = y^{″}(t)$. Find the function $f(t)$."] [17592186176388 :question/problem "The second-order differential equation $y^{″} + 6y^{′} - y = \\sin t$ can be expressed as the matrix equation $x^{′}(t) = Ax(t) + f(t)$, where\n\n| $x(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}, A =$ |  | $0$ | {{field-1}} |  | $, f(t) =$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | $1$ | {{field-3}} |  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |\n\nand for $y = y(t)$, we have $y = x_{1}(t)$ and $y^{′} = x_{2}(t)$."] [17592186176389 :question/problem "The third-order differential equation $y^{‴} - ty^{″} + e^{t}y^{′} = 2$ can be expressed as the matrix equation $x^{′}(t) = Ax(t) + f(t)$, where\n\n| $x(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ x_{3}\\end{bmatrix}, A =$ |  | $0$ | $1$ | {{field-1}} |  | $, f(t) =$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | $0$ | $0$ | {{field-3}} |  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |\n|  | $0$ | $-e^{t}$ | {{field-5}} |  |  | {{field-6}} |  |  |  |\n\nand for $y = y(t)$, we have $y = x_{1}(t)$, $y^{′} = x_{2}(t)$, and $y^{″} = x_{3}(t)$."] [17592186175756 :question/problem "The second-order differential equation\n$y'' - 7t y' + e^{t}y= -t$\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n${x_{1}^{′} = x_{2}; x_{2}^{′} = f(t)$\nwhere $x_1(t) = y(t)$ and $x_2(t) = y'(t)$. Find the function $f(t)$."] [17592186175756 :question/worked-solution "First, we make the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1}(t) &= y \\\\\nx_{2}(t) &= y^{′}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1}^{′}(t) &= y^{′} \\\\\nx_{2}^{′}(t) &= y^{″}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n{x_{1}^{′} = x_{2},; x_{2}^{′} = y^{″}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we're almost done. We just need to write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of $x_1$ and $x_2$.\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $(y'')$, we obtain\n\n$$\ny'' = 7t y' - e^{t}y - t\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\ny'' = 7t x_2 -e^{t}x_1 - t\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side.\n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{x_{1}^{′} = x_{2}; x_{2}^{′} = 7tx_{2} - e^{t}x_{1} - t\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nf(t) = -e^{t}x_1 + 7t x_2 - t\n$$"] [17592186175769 :question/problem "Consider the third-order differential equation\n$y''' + t y' - 8 y = 1 - t^2$,\nwhere $y = y(t)$. If\n${y = x_{1}(t); y^{′} = x_{2}(t); y^{″} = x_{3}(t)$,\nthen the given differential equation can be rewritten as the matrix equation $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t) + \\mathbf f(t)$, where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ x_{3}\\end{bmatrix}$\nDetermine the matrices $A$ and $\\mathbf{f}(t)$."] [17592186175769 :question/worked-solution "We first need to express the given differential equation as a system of first-order differential equations. Then, we write the system as a matrix equation.\n\nWe start by making the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1} &= y \\\\\nx_{2} &= y^{′} \\\\\nx_{3} &= y^{″}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1}^{′} &= y^{′} \\\\\nx_{2}^{′} &= y^{″} \\\\\nx_{3}^{′} &= y^{‴}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n{x_{1}^{′} = x_{2}; x_{2}^{′} = x_{3}; x_{3}^{′} = y^{‴}\n$$\n\nWe must now write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of $x_1, x_2$ and $x_3$.\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $(y''')$, we obtain\n\n$$\ny''' =-t y' + 8 y + 1 - t^2\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\ny''' =-t x_2 + 8 x_1 + 1 - t^2\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side.\n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{x_{1}^{′} = x_{2}; x_{2}^{′} = x_{3}; x_{3}^{′} = 8x_{1} - tx_{2} + 1 - t^{2}\n$$\n\nWe can represent this system as the matrix differential equation\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x'(t) = A\\mathbf x(t) + \\mathbf f(t)\n$$\n\nas follows:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1}^{′} \\\\ x_{2}^{′} \\\\ x_{3}^{′}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 8 & -t & 0\\end{bmatrix}$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186173527 17592186173528 17592186173529 17592186173530 17592186173531] "immutable_fields" [[17592186173526 :answer-field/correct] [17592186173526 :answer-field/id] [17592186173526 :answer-field/key] [17592186173526 :answer-field/type] [17592186174872 :answer-field/id] [17592186174872 :answer-field/key] [17592186174872 :answer-field/type] [17592186174873 :answer-field/id] [17592186174873 :answer-field/key] [17592186174873 :answer-field/type] [17592186174874 :answer-field/id] [17592186174874 :answer-field/key] [17592186174874 :answer-field/type] [17592186174875 :answer-field/id] [17592186174875 :answer-field/key] [17592186174875 :answer-field/type] [17592186174876 :answer-field/id] [17592186174876 :answer-field/key] [17592186174876 :answer-field/type] [17592186174877 :answer-field/id] [17592186174877 :answer-field/key] [17592186174877 :answer-field/type] [17592186174878 :answer-field/id] [17592186174878 :answer-field/key] [17592186174878 :answer-field/type] [17592186174879 :answe